- Story & Context: Trapped under a wet nylon tarp on the rooftop shingles of Barking Creek, Julian spent three sleepless nights attempting to align his cracked Barlow lens with Orion's belt while an idling Dutch container ship sounded its horn every ninety seconds. The vibration shook his fingers raw against the harpsichord’s copper-wound strings, inspiring him to weave the ship’s low B-flat warning drone into a hypnotic, cyclical grunge progression that refused to resolve for nine suffocating minutes. When harbor police climbed the fire escape with searchlights to cut their power, Julian spun the monitors outward and let loose a wall of feedback-drenched breakbeats that sent the patrol skiff rocking in the wash. Pressing his salt-stung forehead against the harpsichord lid, he murmured into the distortion, "I steer by the ballast, never by the sky," as the low-end vibration rattled the floodlights loose.
