- Story & Context: Long after the Purfleet squats were bulldozed into the mud, legends lingered among bait-diggers about the rusted tin shack on Canvey Island where Julian Kestrel vanished during an unmapped sea squall. Witnesses claimed that whenever the estuary fog bank rolled in thick enough to taste copper on the teeth, the deepwater terminal's automated horn system began triggering an unearthly musical response across the corrugated roofs. It was the phantom acoustic signature of an unmanned harpsichord hammering an infinite 165-BPM arpeggio through blown tubes, perfectly greeting each passing cargo hull in the gloom. Night-watch sailors still whisper of hearing a ghostly vocal loop riding the sub-bass spray, howling "we trade the dark above for the dark below," right before the river swallows their radar screens whole.
